.TH "VIRTUALBRICKS\-VBP" "5" "2026\-10\-08" "virtualbricks 3.0.0.dev3" "Virtualbricks Manual"
.SH NAME
virtualbricks\-vbp \- the archive of a Virtualbricks project
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[I]project\f[R].vbp
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \f[I].vbp\f[R] file is a project of Virtualbricks in one file, to move
it to another computer, to keep it or to share it.
The Export window writes it, and the Import window makes a new project
of it; both leave the work to the archive process, described in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-archive\f[R](7).
.PP
It is a tar archive of the project\(aqs folder: the project file, its
README, the private disks of the virtual machines, the other files of
the folder that you chose and, if you chose them too, the disk images
that the disks use, under \f[I]IMAGES/\f[R].
Its first member, \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], lists the others.
.PP
The format follows from four aims:
.IP \(bu 2
Any \f[B]tar\f[R](1) reads and writes it: an archive can be looked into,
or made, without Virtualbricks.
.IP \(bu 2
An archive can take gigabytes, but its first kilobytes say what it
holds: the list and the project file come first.
.IP \(bu 2
The disks keep their holes, and are compressed one by one, in a format
that QEMU runs as it is.
.IP \(bu 2
The project names its images, and the archive stores each one under its
name, not under its file: the computer that imports the archive chooses
where each image goes.
.PP
The archive of a project with one virtual machine, whose disk
\f[B]hda\f[R] is a private disk on top of the image \f[B]debian\f[R],
and whose disk \f[B]hdb\f[R] is the image \f[B]scratch\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
$ tar \-tvf lab.vbp
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- alice/alice     318 2026\-10\-07 22:57 contents.toml
\-rw\-\-\-\-\-\-\- alice/alice    6247 2026\-10\-07 21:19 project.toml
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- alice/alice      31 2026\-10\-07 21:19 README.md
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- alice/alice  328192 2026\-10\-07 22:57 vm1_hda.cow
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- alice/alice  328192 2026\-10\-07 22:57 IMAGES/debian
\-rw\-r\-\-r\-\- alice/alice 33554432 2026\-10\-07 21:19 IMAGES/scratch
.EE
.PP
The examples of this page come from that archive.
.SH THE CONTAINER
.SS Tar
An archive is a tar file in the POSIX.1\-2001 format, \f[I]pax\f[R]: a
sequence of 512\-byte blocks.
Each member is a header block followed by its data, padded with zeros to
a whole block.
Before a member whose attributes don\(aqt fit its header, as times with
fractions of a second or the records of a sparse file, comes a pax
extended header: a member of type \f[B]x\f[R] whose data are records of
the form \f[I]length\f[R] \f[I]key\f[R]=\f[I]value\f[R], each ending
with a newline.
Two blocks of zeros end the archive, which is then padded with zeros to
a multiple of 10240 bytes, the record of tar.
.PP
The blocks of the example:
.IP
.EX
 offset  type  header name                       size
      0  x     PaxHeader/contents.toml             90
   1024  0     contents.toml                      318
   2048  x     PaxHeader/project.toml              90
   3072  0     project.toml                      6247
  10240  x     PaxHeader/README.md                 90
  11264  0     README.md                           31
  12288  x     PaxHeader/vm1_hda.cow              195
  13312  0     GNUSparseFile.0/vm1_hda.cow     267264
 281088  x     IMAGES/PaxHeader/debian            197
 282112  0     IMAGES/GNUSparseFile.0/debian   267264
 549888  x     IMAGES/PaxHeader/scratch           200
 550912  0     IMAGES/GNUSparseFile.0/scratch    4608
 556032        14 blocks of zeros, to 563200
.EE
.PP
The members with holes are GNU sparse members, whose header name is a
placeholder; see \f[B]HOLES\f[R].
.SS Compression
When \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] is installed, the archive is not compressed as
a whole: its disks are compressed one by one inside it (see \f[B]PACKED
DISKS\f[R]), and the rest is a few kilobytes.
Without \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R], the whole archive is compressed with gzip,
whose header then has no time.
.PP
Virtualbricks reads an archive that is not compressed or compressed with
gzip, bzip2 or xz, which it recognizes by its first bytes, whatever its
extension.
.SS The name of the file
An archive has the extension \f[I].vbp\f[R], compressed or not.
The Import window offers the files \f[I]*.vbp\f[R], \f[I]*.tar.gz\f[R],
\f[I]*.tgz\f[R] and \f[I]*.tar\f[R].
The name of the file suggests the name of the project: without
\f[I].vbp\f[R], \f[I].tar.gz\f[R], \f[I].tgz\f[R], \f[I].tar.xz\f[R],
\f[I].tar.bz2\f[R] or its last extension, at most 38 bytes, without
leading dots, and the next free name, \f[I]lab\-2\f[R],
\f[I]lab\-3\f[R]...
when the workspace has a project with that name.
The archive itself doesn\(aqt name the project.
.SS Names and attributes
Every member is a regular file, named by its path relative to the
project\(aqs folder, with \f[I]/\f[R] between folders.
A name has no leading \f[I]/\f[R] and no \f[I]..\f[R]; a reader drops a
leading \f[I]./\f[R] and a trailing \f[I]/\f[R], so that an archive made
with \f[B]tar \-C\f[R] \f[I]folder\f[R] \f[B].\f[R] works too.
The members that aren\(aqt regular files, such as the folders of such an
archive, are skipped by an inspection; links and devices are never
written, and the import skips them when it extracts the archive with
Python\(aqs \f[B]tarfile\f[R].
.PP
The owner, the group, the permissions and the times of the members are
those that the writer found; Virtualbricks gives them no meaning.
The writers differ in details that a reader mustn\(aqt depend on:
.TP
\f[B]bsdtar\f[R]
Pax headers named \f[I]folder\f[R]/PaxHeader/\f[I]name\f[R], with the
records \f[B]ctime\f[R], \f[B]atime\f[R] and \f[B]mtime\f[R]; sparse
members named \f[I]folder\f[R]/GNUSparseFile.0/\f[I]name\f[R].
.TP
GNU \f[B]tar\f[R]
Pax headers named \f[I]folder\f[R]/PaxHeaders/\f[I]name\f[R], with
\f[B]mtime\f[R], \f[B]atime\f[R] and \f[B]ctime\f[R]; sparse members
named \f[I]folder\f[R]/GNUSparseFile.\f[I]pid\f[R]/\f[I]name\f[R], after
the process number of \f[B]tar\f[R].
.TP
Python\(aqs \f[B]tarfile\f[R], in Virtualbricks
Pax headers named \f[I]././\(atPaxHeader\f[R], with \f[B]mtime\f[R]
only; sparse members named as by \f[B]bsdtar\f[R]; no owner and group
names.
.SH MEMBERS
The name of a member gives its \f[I]kind\f[R], the word in italics,
which the archive process uses in its messages:
.TP
\f[B]contents.toml\f[R]
\f[I]contents\f[R]: the list of the other members; see
\f[B]CONTENTS.TOML\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]project.toml\f[R]
\f[I]project\f[R]: the project file, described in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5); see \f[B]THE PROJECT FILE\f[R].
.TP
\f[B].project\f[R], \f[B].project\(ti\f[R]
\f[I]legacy project\f[R]: the project file of Virtualbricks 2.1 and
older; see \f[B]OLDER ARCHIVES\f[R].
An archive with both project files is read from \f[B]project.toml\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]README.md\f[R], \f[B]README\f[R]
\f[I]readme\f[R]: the description of the project, plain text in UTF\-8
that Virtualbricks reads as Markdown (see \f[B]README\f[R] in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5)).
Before 3.0 it was \f[B]README\f[R], which the import renames to
\f[B]README.md\f[R]; in an archive with both, \f[B]README.md\f[R] is the
README.
.TP
\f[I]vm\f[R]_\f[I]device\f[R].cow
\f[I]disk\f[R]: the private disk of the device \f[I]device\f[R] of the
virtual machine \f[I]vm\f[R], where \f[I]device\f[R] is \f[B]hda\f[R],
\f[B]hdb\f[R], \f[B]hdc\f[R], \f[B]hdd\f[R], \f[B]fda\f[R],
\f[B]fdb\f[R] or \f[B]mtdblock\f[R]: the part after the last underscore,
so \f[I]router_1_hda.cow\f[R] is the disk \f[B]hda\f[R] of
\f[B]router_1\f[R].
Only at the top of the archive.
See \f[B]PRIVATE DISKS\f[R].
.TP
IMAGES/\f[I]image\f[R]
\f[I]image\f[R]: the disk image that the project file calls
\f[I]image\f[R], in \f[B][images.\f[R]\f[I]image\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R].
The member is named after the image, not after its file: the image
\f[B]debian\f[R] whose file is \f[I]/srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2\f[R] is the
member \f[I]IMAGES/debian\f[R].
See \f[B]IMAGES AND DISKS\f[R].
The archives of Virtualbricks 2.1 and of the development versions of 3.0
have it in \f[I].images/\f[R], which is read the same way.
.TP
any other name
\f[I]other\f[R]: a file of the project\(aqs folder that you chose to
export, as a picture of the README, \f[I]pictures/map.png\f[R].
The import puts it back where it was.
.PP
An archive never holds what older versions left in a project\(aqs
folder: its \f[I].images\f[R] folder and its \f[I].project\f[R] files.
Nor does it hold a folder \f[I]IMAGES\f[R] of the project, whose files
an import would take for images: the export leaves it out, and says so.
.SH ORDER
A new archive starts with \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], then the project file
and the README: together, the \f[I]head\f[R] of the archive.
The other files, the private disks and the images follow, in this order,
each group from the smallest file to the largest:
.IP
.EX
 +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
 | contents | project | README | other | disks | IMAGES/  |
 |   .toml  |  .toml  |  .md   | files |       |          |
 +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
 |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- head \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<\- smallest to largest \-\->|
     read by an inspection          read by the import
.EE
.PP
The order is the writer\(aqs promise; a reader accepts any order.
With \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] first, Virtualbricks reads the archive
until it has the members of the head that the list names, and stops
there, whatever the size of the archive.
Without \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], it reads the archive to its end.
.SH CONTENTS.TOML
The list of the other members, in the order of the archive, in TOML 1.0
and UTF\-8.
The list of the example:
.IP
.EX
format = 1

[[members]]
name = \(dqproject.toml\(dq
size = 6247

[[members]]
name = \(dqREADME.md\(dq
size = 31

[[members]]
name = \(dqvm1_hda.cow\(dq
size = 328192
packed = true
real_size = 851968

[[members]]
name = \(dqIMAGES/debian\(dq
size = 328192
packed = true
real_size = 1376256

[[members]]
name = \(dqIMAGES/scratch\(dq
size = 33554432
.EE
.TP
\f[B]format\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
The version of the layout, \f[B]1\f[R].
An archive with a higher \f[B]format\f[R] is not read:
\(dqcontents.toml: written by a newer Virtualbricks (format 2)\(dq.
The format changes only for what an older reader can\(aqt ignore: keys
it doesn\(aqt know are ignored.
.TP
\f[B]members\f[R] = \f[I]array of tables\f[R]
One table for each member but \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] itself:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The name of the member, as in its header.
.TP
\f[B]size\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
The size of the file that the member holds, holes included: what \f[B]ls
\-l\f[R] says once it\(aqs extracted.
A sparse member takes fewer bytes in the archive:
\f[B]IMAGES/scratch\f[R] takes 4608 bytes for its 32 MiB.
.TP
\f[B]packed\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R]
Present, and \f[B]true\f[R], only when \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] compressed
the member; see \f[B]PACKED DISKS\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]real_size\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
Only with \f[B]packed\f[R]: the size of the disk before it was
compressed, the size it has on the computer it comes from.
.RE
.PP
The kind of a member comes from its name and is not written.
An item of \f[B]members\f[R] that isn\(aqt a table is skipped, a size
that isn\(aqt an integer is read as 0, a \f[B]packed\f[R] that isn\(aqt
\f[B]true\f[R] is false, and other keys are ignored.
.SH THE PROJECT FILE
The \f[B]project.toml\f[R] of an archive is the project file as the
project had it, in the format of the Virtualbricks that exported it: 2
today.
The import upgrades an older one, and refuses a newer one:
\(dqproject.toml: written by a newer Virtualbricks (format 3)\(dq.
An archive carries the paths of the computer it comes from, in these
keys, which the import changes:
.TP
\f[B][images.\f[R]\f[I]name\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R] \f[B]path\f[R]
The file of the image on the computer it comes from: absolute, or
relative to the project\(aqs folder.
The import writes the file that the image uses on this computer, or
\(dq\(dq for an image left unset.
.TP
\f[B][bricks.\f[R]\f[I]vm\f[R]\f[B].disks.\f[R]\f[I]device\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R] \f[B]image\f[R], \f[B]private\f[R]
The image of a disk, by name, and whether the disk writes to its private
disk, \f[I]vm\f[R]_\f[I]device\f[R].cow.
The import changes neither.
.TP
\f[B][settings]\f[R] \f[B]qemu_path\f[R], \f[B]vde_path\f[R]
The folders of QEMU and VDE on the computer it comes from.
The import replaces each with this computer\(aqs when the folder
doesn\(aqt exist here, unless you choose otherwise.
.PP
The rest of the file is imported as it is.
The private disks carry a path too: see \f[B]PRIVATE DISKS\f[R].
.SH IMAGES AND DISKS
A disk doesn\(aqt name a file: it names an image of the project, and the
image has the path of its file.
The archive stores the image under that name, so the name ties the three
together:
.IP
.EX
 project.toml                          the archive
 +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
 | [bricks.vm1.disks.hda]            |
 | image = \(dqdebian\(dq \-\-.              |
 | private = true     |              |
 |                    v              |  the name
 | [images.debian] <\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-> IMAGES/debian
 | path = \(dq/srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2\(dq  |
 +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
            \(ha
            \(aq\-\-\-\-\- backing file \-\-\-\-\-\- vm1_hda.cow
.EE
.PP
The import plans an image for each table
\f[B][images.\f[R]\f[I]name\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R] of the project file.
Its disks are those whose \f[B]image\f[R] is \f[I]name\f[R], and its
copy in the archive is the member \f[I]IMAGES/name\f[R], if the archive
has it.
The Import window gives each image a choice, with a default:
.IP \(bu 2
An image that the archive has is copied into the shared images,
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/shared_images, under the name of its file on the
computer it comes from, \f[I]debian\-12.qcow2\f[R], or the next free
one, \f[I]debian\-12.1.qcow2\f[R]...
When the shared images have a file with that name and the size of the
image, the \f[B]real_size\f[R] of a packed one, that file is used
instead.
.IP \(bu 2
An image that the archive doesn\(aqt have uses its file if this computer
has it at the same path, otherwise a file of the shared images with the
name of its file or of the image; otherwise it\(aqs left unset, and its
disks get an image later, in their settings.
.PP
Then the import writes the path of each image into
\f[B][images.\f[R]\f[I]name\f[R]\f[B]]\f[R], points the private disks at
it (see \f[B]PRIVATE DISKS\f[R]), and removes the \f[I]IMAGES\f[R]
folder.
A member of \f[I]IMAGES/\f[R] that no table of the project file names is
dropped.
By default, a file of the shared images with the file name and the size
of an image is taken for it: nothing compares their contents.
.PP
The Export window stores every image of the project whose file exists,
or none; it stores none by default, as images are large and often
already on the other computer.
.SH PRIVATE DISKS
A disk with \f[B]private\f[R] = \f[B]true\f[R] writes to
\f[I]vm\f[R]_\f[I]device\f[R].cow in the project\(aqs folder, a qcow2
file on top of the image, which stays unchanged.
The private disk names its image by the image\(aqs file, as its
\f[I]backing file\f[R]: a path of the computer it comes from, kept in
the archive, packed or not:
.IP
.EX
$ qemu\-img info vm1_hda.cow
image: vm1_hda.cow
file format: qcow2
virtual size: 64 MiB (67108864 bytes)
disk size: 264 KiB
cluster_size: 65536
backing file: /srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2
backing file format: qcow2
Format specific information:
    compat: 1.1
    compression type: zstd
\&...
.EE
.PP
The import points each private disk at the file that its image uses on
this computer, in the format of that file:
.IP
.EX
qemu\-img rebase \-u \-b image \-F format disk
.EE
.PP
\f[B]\-u\f[R], the unsafe mode, changes only the name of the backing
file: the image must hold the same data as the one the disk was made on.
A private disk whose image is left unset keeps its backing file as it
is, and the import says so.
A private disk made by an older version may be in another format than
qcow2: it\(aqs stored as it is, and the import points it at its image as
the others.
.SH PACKED DISKS
When \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] is installed, the export compresses each
private disk and each image that is in the qcow2 format:
.IP
.EX
qemu\-img convert \-p \-O qcow2 \-m 8 \-W \(rs
    \-c \-o compression_type=zstd \(rs
    [\-B backing \-F backing\-format] disk packed\-disk
.EE
.PP
with zlib, without \f[B]\-o compression_type=zstd\f[R], when QEMU is
older than 5.1.
A packed disk is a valid qcow2 file whose clusters are compressed, and
QEMU runs it as it is; a private disk keeps its backing file.
The member is marked \f[B]packed\f[R] in \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], with
its \f[B]real_size\f[R].
A disk or an image in another format, as raw, is stored as it is; so is
one that \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] can\(aqt read, as a private disk whose
image is gone, and the export says so.
.PP
The import converts the packed members back into normal qcow2 files,
with \f[B]qemu\-img convert\f[R] without \f[B]\-c\f[R]: the images it
copies, and the private disks on top of their new image.
The others stay compressed, and work as they are: a private disk whose
image is left unset, any packed member when \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] isn\(aqt
installed.
An archive without \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] has no packed member.
.SH HOLES
A disk is a sparse file: a disk of 20 GB can take 2 GB of space.
The archive keeps the holes of its members, as GNU sparse 1.0 members,
which \f[B]bsdtar\f[R], GNU \f[B]tar\f[R] with \f[B]\-\-sparse\f[R] and
Virtualbricks itself write.
A sparse member is:
.IP \(bu 2
a pax extended header with the records \f[B]GNU.sparse.major=1\f[R],
\f[B]GNU.sparse.minor=0\f[R], \f[B]GNU.sparse.name\f[R], the name of the
member, and \f[B]GNU.sparse.realsize\f[R], the size of the file with its
holes;
.IP \(bu 2
a header whose name is a placeholder, \f[I]GNUSparseFile.0/name\f[R] or
the like, which a reader replaces with \f[B]GNU.sparse.name\f[R], and
whose size is that of the data that follow;
.IP \(bu 2
the map of the data: decimal numbers, each ending with a newline, the
number of regions, then the offset and the length of each, padded with
zeros to a whole block;
.IP \(bu 2
the data of the regions, one after the other.
.PP
A file that ends with a hole has a last region of length 0, at its size.
The member \f[I]IMAGES/scratch\f[R] of the example, a file of 32 MiB
with 4096 bytes of data at 4 MiB, takes 4608 bytes: one block of map,
then the data.
Its map:
.IP
.EX
2
4194304
4096
33554432
0
.EE
.PP
A reader that doesn\(aqt know the format extracts
\f[I]IMAGES/GNUSparseFile.0/scratch\f[R], a file with the map and the
data, which isn\(aqt the image.
.PP
A header holds sizes up to 8 GiB, in octal.
\f[B]bsdtar\f[R] and GNU \f[B]tar\f[R] write a larger size as a
\f[B]size\f[R] record of the pax header; Virtualbricks, when it writes
the archive itself, writes it in the header, in base 256: the first byte
0x80, then the size, big\-endian.
Python\(aqs \f[B]tarfile\f[R] misreads a sparse member with a
\f[B]size\f[R] record; see \f[B]BUGS\f[R].
.PP
When the import extracts an archive, \f[B]bsdtar \-S\f[R] turns runs of
zeros into holes, also those that the archive didn\(aqt keep.
After GNU \f[B]tar\f[R] or \f[B]tarfile\f[R], the import does the same
for the private disks and the copied images: blocks of 64 KiB of zeros
become holes, if they add up to 1 MiB at least.
.SH OLDER ARCHIVES
Virtualbricks 2.1 exported a project with \f[B]bsdtar\f[R] or
\f[B]tar\f[R], \f[B]cfzh\f[R]: a tar of the project\(aqs folder
compressed with gzip, without \f[B]contents.toml\f[R].
It holds the files you chose, then the project file, \f[B].project\f[R],
and the \f[B]README\f[R], then the images, under
\f[I].images/image\f[R]: the export made a folder of links named after
the images, which tar followed.
The private disks are \f[I]vm\f[R]_\f[I]device\f[R].cow, as today.
.PP
The \f[B].project\f[R] file is the project file of those versions, which
the import converts as the migration does (see \f[B]MIGRATION\f[R] in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5)):
.IP
.EX
[Image:debian]
path=/srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2

[Qemu:vm1]
hda=debian
privatehda=*
.EE
.PP
As the project file comes near the end, an inspection reads such an
archive to its end.
It sends what it knows as soon as it has read the project file, and the
full list of members at the end; the Import window shows its form from
the first, and the import starts without waiting for the second.
.SH READING AN ARCHIVE
Virtualbricks reads an archive as a stream, once, from its start:
.IP "1." 3
It recognizes the compression from the first bytes: gzip, bzip2, xz, or
none.
.IP "2." 3
It skips the members that aren\(aqt regular files.
It takes the name of a sparse member from \f[B]GNU.sparse.name\f[R],
drops a leading \f[I]./\f[R] and a trailing \f[I]/\f[R], and gets the
kind of the member from its name.
.IP "3." 3
It reads the members of the head: \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], the project
file, the README.
It refuses a \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] of a newer format.
.IP "4." 3
With \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], it stops when it has every member of the
head that the list names.
Without it, it reads on to the end, and lists the members from their
headers.
.IP "5." 3
It reads \f[B]project.toml\f[R], or converts \f[B].project\f[R], and
fails when there is neither.
\f[B]README.md\f[R] comes before \f[B]README\f[R].
.PP
To import an archive, it extracts it whole, into a hidden folder of the
workspace, and goes on as \f[B]virtualbricks\-archive\f[R](7) describes.
.SH WRITING AN ARCHIVE
A file that Virtualbricks imports needs only a project file.
An archive that it reads as well as its own:
.IP \(bu 2
is a pax tar file, not compressed when its disks are packed, otherwise
compressed with gzip;
.IP \(bu 2
starts with \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], of format 1, listing every other
member, then has the project file and the README;
.IP \(bu 2
names the members after their path in the project\(aqs folder, the
images \f[I]IMAGES/name\f[R] after their name in the project file;
.IP \(bu 2
keeps the holes of the disks;
.IP \(bu 2
packs the qcow2 disks, keeping their backing files, and marks them
\f[B]packed\f[R], with their \f[B]real_size\f[R].
.PP
The backing file of a private disk can be any path: the import replaces
it, and its format, with the file that the image uses on this computer.
.SH EXAMPLES
Look into an archive without extracting it:
.IP
.EX
$ tar \-xOf lab.vbp contents.toml
$ tar \-xOf lab.vbp README.md
$ tar \-xOf lab.vbp project.toml |
>     grep \-e \(aq\(ha\(rs[images\(aq \-e \(aq\(hapath =\(aq
[images.debian]
path = \(dq/srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2\(dq
[images.scratch]
path = \(dq/srv/vm/scratch.img\(dq
.EE
.PP
Extract a private disk, and see its image and its compression:
.IP
.EX
$ tar \-xf lab.vbp vm1_hda.cow
$ qemu\-img info vm1_hda.cow | grep \-e backing \-e compression
backing file: /srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2
backing file format: qcow2
    compression type: zstd
.EE
.PP
Point it at the image of this computer, by hand, as the import does:
.IP
.EX
$ qemu\-img rebase \-u \-F qcow2 \(rs
>     \-b \(ti/.virtualbricks/shared_images/debian\-12.qcow2 \(rs
>     vm1_hda.cow
.EE
.PP
Make an archive by hand with GNU \f[B]tar\f[R], keeping the holes, with
the image \f[B]debian\f[R] under its name.
Virtualbricks imports it, after reading it to its end, as it has no
\f[B]contents.toml\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
$ tar \-c \-\-sparse \-\-format=posix \-f lab.vbp \(rs
>     \-C \(ti/.virtualbricks/lab \(rs
>     project.toml README.md vm1_hda.cow \(rs
>     \-C /srv/vm \(rs
>     \-\-transform \(aqs,\(hadebian\-12\(rs.qcow2$,IMAGES/debian,\(aq \(rs
>     debian\-12.qcow2
$ tar \-tf lab.vbp
project.toml
README.md
vm1_hda.cow
IMAGES/debian
.EE
.PP
The archive of the whole folder of a project, compressed with gzip,
which doesn\(aqt keep the holes of its disks:
.IP
.EX
$ tar \-czf \(ti/lab.vbp \-C \(ti/.virtualbricks/lab .
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]name\f[R].vbp
An archive.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/shared_images/
The shared images, where the import copies the images.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/\f[I]name\f[R]/
The project that the import makes of an archive.
.SH BUGS
Python\(aqs \f[B]tarfile\f[R], from 3.10 to 3.13, misreads a GNU sparse
1.0 member whose size is in a pax record, as \f[B]bsdtar\f[R] and GNU
\f[B]tar\f[R] write the members larger than 8 GiB, and loses the member
after it.
Virtualbricks then reads the archive with \f[B]bsdtar\f[R] or GNU
\f[B]tar\f[R]; with neither, it can\(aqt read it.
